\section*{Bounded additive gap for the bin-packing configuration LP}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 118; 2026-09-24), the asserted result is that bin-packing configuration relaxations have arbitrarily large additive gaps, and distinguishing $B$ bins from more than $B+c$ is NP-hard for each fixed $c$, even with all items larger than $1/6$.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
Let $\operatorname{LP}(I)$ be the fractional configuration-LP optimum for unit-capacity bin packing and $\operatorname{OPT}(I)$ the minimum integer bin count. For each integer $c\ge0$, the manuscript claims a finite rational instance $I$ and integer $B$ with every item larger than $1/6$ and
\[
 \operatorname{LP}(I)=B,\qquad\operatorname{OPT}(I)>B+c.
\]
The same fractional value holds if each pattern is required to be a subset of the individual item copies. Hence the additive configuration gap is unbounded.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 118; 2026-09-24), a vertex-cover instance is encoded by rational item labels whose feasible tuples are explicitly controlled. Fractional patterns have value $B$, but extracting any integral packing gives a small vertex cover; a separate finite witness yields the unconditional unbounded gap.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The displayed question has a negative answer under the manuscript's stated construction. Its counterexample meets the conditions in this problem and defeats the universal assertion, so this is a full disproof claim. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 118; 2026-09-24).

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 118; 2026-09-24), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: bin-packing configuration relaxations have arbitrarily large additive gaps, and distinguishing $B$ bins from more than $B+c$ is NP-hard for each fixed $c$, even with all items larger than $1/6$.

The selected statement includes the clause used for this problem.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{BinPackingGap.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.BinPackingGap.main\_results}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

